WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.200
The analytical toolkit.

2
00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:07.288
Four reasoning tools that turn vague AI questions into answerable ones.

3
00:00:07.288 --> 00:00:11.267
Fluency with them is the loudest seniority signal there is.

4
00:00:12.467 --> 00:00:16.200
Tool one: Fermi estimation, for can we afford it questions.

5
00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:19.767
Decompose the question into quantities you can bound.

6
00:00:20.067 --> 00:00:22.033
Anchor on numbers you have memorized.

7
00:00:22.333 --> 00:00:23.082
Sanity-check.

8
00:00:23.082 --> 00:00:26.133
You need the right order of magnitude, not precision.

9
00:00:26.433 --> 00:00:28.600
Then name the lever that moves the answer.

10
00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:31.267
Try this drill on paper.

11
00:00:31.567 --> 00:00:35.867
A support bot serves fifty thousand conversations a day, eight turns each.

12
00:00:36.167 --> 00:00:39.500
Each turn is three thousand tokens in, three hundred out.

13
00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:45.733
What is the monthly cost on a frontier model versus a small one, and what cache hit rate makes them equal?

14
00:00:46.933 --> 00:00:49.533
Tool two: hypothesis driven debugging.

15
00:00:49.833 --> 00:00:51.525
Don't just change the prompt.

16
00:00:51.525 --> 00:00:52.400
Localize first.

17
00:00:52.700 --> 00:00:54.533
Was the right chunk retrieved at all?

18
00:00:54.833 --> 00:00:58.957
If not, it is retrieval: embedding, chunking, or the query.

19
00:00:58.957 --> 00:01:03.010
If yes, it is generation: prompt, context order, or model.

20
00:01:03.010 --> 00:01:04.967
One check halves the search.

21
00:01:06.167 --> 00:01:08.100
Tool three: structured tradeoffs.

22
00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:12.900
Score every choice on quality, latency, cost, complexity, and risk.

23
00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:17.067
Reranking spends four hundred milliseconds and fifteen percent cost.

24
00:01:17.367 --> 00:01:23.400
It buys about twenty points of precision, obviously worth it for legal search, not for autocomplete.

25
00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:26.900
Tool four: reasoning about uncertainty.

26
00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:31.667
On fifty examples, differences under about ten points are usually noise.

27
00:01:31.967 --> 00:01:35.667
Fix seeds and temperature, so you measure your change, not the dice.

28
00:01:35.967 --> 00:01:39.733
Compare old and new on identical inputs, item by item.

29
00:01:40.033 --> 00:01:41.254
And beware the tail.

30
00:01:41.254 --> 00:01:43.633
Users feel p ninety five, not the mean.

31
00:01:44.833 --> 00:01:45.662
Let's recap.

32
00:01:45.662 --> 00:01:49.733
Fermi estimation: decompose, anchor, check, name the lever.

33
00:01:50.033 --> 00:01:52.800
Bisect the pipeline, one variable at a time.

34
00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:55.533
Make tradeoffs explicit on five axes.

35
00:01:55.833 --> 00:01:57.852
And respect noise and the tail.

36
00:01:57.852 --> 00:02:00.067
Next up: critical-thinking drills.
